<b>Interview 9: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;： writer and bookseller, male, born in 1950s, Zhejiang</b>

<p><b>What’s the main problem with China’s publication system?</b>
<br>I am a bookseller and writer, book planner, publisher. Selling books is a tough business, nowadays there are not many people still doing it. Government doesn’t open the system. This makes you very passive, because no matter how good a publication plan you have, you must coordinate with official publishing company. There’s too much restriction. You have to pay for the book number and it costs a lot.

<p><b>Is there any way to change it?</b>
<br>I think the only way is to allow private publishing company. Any reformation or any loosing of restriction is of no use unless private publishing company is legalized. Of course government can examine books. Censorship should exist but at least we should have fair competition. For the last twenty years private corporations developed very fast but cultural industry is in stasis, and more and more so. There are fewer and fewer good book sellers. The law is the same with other private business: the more control the more disorder. It’s better not to control. In fact official publishing company and official Xinhua Bookstore are both in such a mess. Xinhua Bookstore was there since the Yan An period, and it’s always monopolized by the government until now.

<p><b>Why do you choose to be a book seller?</b>
<br>I am the first to “plunge into the commercial sea” in Jinghua city commission. I gave up all my cadre identity and files. I just want to go in for literature and art. I am not ambitious. I just like literature history. I published two sets of books in 2000, one of which is by post-70s writers. I got 26 writers then, the first is Sugar by Mian Mian. In Beijing and Shanghai, nobody was willing to publish this book, but I thought it was great, much different from other books. The catchword “new-new human” was actually from me, I had a “new-new human” series. It was “new human” series originally, but because of my stuttering, it became “new-new human” series. Lots of writers stutter including Yu Hua. Fast thoughts, slow words, it’s quite contradictory.

<p><b>Does stuttering cause trouble in your life or does it help you with writing?</b>
<br>There’s no trouble and it helps. Fast thoughts, slow words, that’s why I stutter. I created “beauty authors” too. Shanghai Baby by Wei Hui was a few months earlier than Sugar, back then it got great resound from The 20 Books that Influence Me. Shanghai Baby attracted huge attention and a lot of media was after it. There’s a bad phenomenon in China and in the world too, that is, if it sells better it’s more easily banned, and vice versa. Black books are everywhere. On the other hand, media was not helping either. There’s a reporter called Wu Fei in North Young who had a whole page publishing his article “Alternative Novels Becoming Mainstream Stately?” and with editor’s notes saying that I was interviewed by AP and BBC. I told him that it’s better not to put this on newspaper. Anyway, we were noticed by the government and not surprisingly, the book was banned within a month. What’s more, my “new-new human series” and “beauty author series” were banned altogether. It should be very profitable but in the end I lost a great fortune. This is force majeure.

<p><b>Are there many good cultural publications these years?</b>
<br>Not many. There’s no good publisher. China has no professional publisher. Authors just have to do everything themselves. Businesses such as real estate have government support, but no body cares about bookselling. Publishing company only sells book numbers and bans things if there’s any problem. There’s no plan. And I believe no book seller has ever received any money from the government. If a bookseller has a good idea, he can’t do it if he has no money. If you like books and have a lot of money, then you can do the business. If the books are bad, you lost, if the books are good, you are banned and lost more. We are all very disappointed, so how can we publish good books? Nowadays many booksellers don’t know books. They will do anything as long as it’s profitable. I have done this kind of thing too. The two booksellers who were arrested that year, like the case with Liu Yong, I was actually the mastermind behind. They do authorized books as well as black books. They wanted to expand the business too much but lacked money, and there was no government funding support. This situation forces many ambitious booksellers to do bad things.
<p>Government uses literature as ideology and mainstream propaganda. They never think of planning and developing it as a real business. The country is developing economically, but publishing business is going backwards. Books that could be published in 90s can’t be published now. Once I edited a “former graduates of 1966~1968” series, three parts, one for the red guards, one for the urban youths, and one for those came back to the city for college entrance. But none can be published now. Today there are more kinds of books in the market, but the contents are still very monotony and unitary. There is still too much restriction. 
<p>A really confident party and nation should give everything chances to survive. Even a poisonous plant should be viewed and appreciated by people in some way. “New-new human” was actually taken as a new “cultural movement”. The National Security Bureau investigated me twice, the first time is to check out whether I have any overseas background, the second time is about whether I joined “June 4th” or not. But in fact, the so-called “alternative literature” is merely to borrow a concept. There is alternative music, alternative painting, why can’t there be alternative literature? After the banning the “post-70s writer” series is aborted too. It was stifled in the cradle.
<p>Among those post-70s writers, Mian Mian’s book was published first by me, then Wei Hui’s. Mian Mian’s language is narrative and Wei Hui’s is descriptive. It’s better to combine both together but they all went to extremes. The two of them used to be friends but they broke up. The story of Shanghai Baby was originally a movie plan by Mian Mian but was used by Wei Hui to write her book. They fought in a complete mess in Chengdu, but in the end both their books were sold quite well. Wei Hui’s was sold better. Wei Hui has a better feeling for scenes and Mian Mian is more literary. The first time I met Mian Mian, there were many people present including Mo Yan. She talked for two hours, almost as talkative as Wang Suo. It’s fate that makes people famous, not booksellers.

<p><b>Do you think your personal value is fulfilled?</b>
<br>No I don’t think so.

<p><b>Are you positive or negative towards life?</b>
<br>Negative, absolutely negative.

<p><b>Do you have any faith?</b>
<br>Basically, no. 

